findings.body:
`newResetToken()` now builds the reset secret from `Math.floor(Math.random() * 1e12).toString(36)`, which yields at most ~10^12 values (~40 bits) and is not a CSPRNG. The previous `randomBytes(32)` path produced 256 bits of secure entropy as a 64-char hex string.

If an attacker can trigger or observe reset flows, they can brute-force or predict this token and take over accounts. Edge runtimes still expose Web Crypto — use that and keep a 32-byte token.

```suggestion
  const bytes = new Uint8Array(32);
  crypto.getRandomValues(bytes);
  return Array.from(bytes, (b) => b.toString(16).padStart(2, "0")).join("");
```

Verified against base `src/auth/reset.ts` (was `randomBytes(32).toString("hex")`) and MDN/Edge Web Crypto availability of `crypto.getRandomValues`.
